(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-03"] = {"3.1":{"s":"A trigonometric equation holds only for certain angles. Because sine, cosine and tangent repeat, one solution gives infinitely many; the formula that lists them all, using an integer n, is the general solution. The principal solutions are those between 0 and 2π. Standard results cover sin, cos and tan of θ equal to those of a known angle α, and their squares.","f":[{"x":"sin(theta) = sin(alpha) -> theta = n*pi + (-1)^n*alpha","n":"General solution; n is any integer.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.68"},{"x":"cos(theta) = cos(alpha) -> theta = 2*n*pi +- alpha","n":"General solution of the cosine equation.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.68"},{"x":"tan(theta) = tan(alpha) -> theta = n*pi + alpha","n":"General solution of the tangent equation.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.68"},{"x":"sin^2(theta) = sin^2(alpha) -> theta = n*pi +- alpha","n":"Same form for cos^2 and tan^2.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.69"}],"w":["sin θ = 0 gives θ = nπ, but cos θ = 0 gives θ = (2n + 1)π/2.","Do not cancel a factor like sin θ from both sides: you lose the solutions where it is zero."],"x":["A trigonometric equation is true only for some angles. Because sine, cosine and tangent repeat, one solution leads to infinitely many. The solutions between 0 and 2π are the principal solutions. The formula that lists all of them, with an integer n, is the general solution.","Standard results. sin θ = sin α gives θ = nπ + (−1)ⁿα. cos θ = cos α gives θ = 2nπ ± α. tan θ = tan α gives θ = nπ + α. For squares, sin²θ = sin²α, cos²θ = cos²α and tan²θ = tan²α all give θ = nπ ± α.","Special cases: sin θ = 0 gives θ = nπ. cos θ = 0 gives θ = (2n + 1)π/2. tan θ = 0 gives θ = nπ. sin θ = 1 gives θ = 2nπ + π/2. cos θ = 1 gives θ = 2nπ.","Method: (1) bring the equation to one trigonometric function, using identities such as sin²θ = 1 − cos²θ; (2) if it is a quadratic, factorise; (3) write each factor equal to zero; (4) for each, find a known angle α and use the standard result.","Common mistakes: cancelling a common factor such as sin θ from both sides (this loses the solutions where it is zero; take it to one side and factorise instead), and using 2nπ ± α for sine or tangent."],"e":"Solve 2sin²θ − sin θ − 1 = 0. Factorise: (2 sin θ + 1)(sin θ − 1) = 0. So sin θ = 1, giving θ = 2nπ + π/2, or sin θ = −1/2 = sin(−π/6), giving θ = nπ + (−1)ⁿ(−π/6)."},"3.2":{"s":"Solving a triangle means finding its unknown sides and angles from the known ones (at least one must be a side). In triangle ABC the sides opposite A, B, C are a, b, c. The sine rule, cosine rule and projection rule link them. From these come the half-angle formulas in terms of the semi-perimeter s, Heron's formula for the area, and Napier's analogy.","f":[{"x":"a/sin(A) = b/sin(B) = c/sin(C) = 2*R","n":"Sine rule; R = circumradius.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.77"},{"x":"a^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2*b*c*cos(A)","n":"Cosine rule (likewise for b^2 and c^2).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.79"},{"x":"a = b*cos(C) + c*cos(B)","n":"Projection rule (likewise for b and c).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.81"},{"x":"cos(A/2) = sqrt(s*(s - a)/(b*c))","n":"Half-angle formula; s = (a + b + c)/2. Also sin(A/2) = sqrt((s - b)(s - c)/(b c)).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.82"},{"x":"A = sqrt(s*(s - a)*(s - b)*(s - c))","n":"Heron's formula for the area of the triangle.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.84"},{"x":"tan((B - C)/2) = ((b - c)/(b + c))*cot(A/2)","n":"Napier's analogy.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.84"}],"w":["The cosine rule gives an angle from three sides; the sine rule needs a side-angle pair."],"x":["In a triangle ABC, the sides opposite A, B, C are a, b, c, and s = (a + b + c)/2. Solving a triangle means finding the unknown sides and angles from the known ones. At least one side must be known.","Sine rule: a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C = 2R, where R is the circumradius. Use it when you know a side with its opposite angle. Cosine rule: a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A, so cos A = (b² + c² − a²)/(2bc). Use it for two sides with the angle between them, or for all three sides. Projection rule: a = b cos C + c cos B, and similarly for b and c.","Half-angle formulas: sin(A/2) = √((s − b)(s − c)/(bc)), cos(A/2) = √(s(s − a)/(bc)), and tan(A/2) = √((s − b)(s − c)/(s(s − a))). Area: Δ = (1/2)bc sin A, or by Heron's formula Δ = √(s(s − a)(s − b)(s − c)). Napier's analogy: tan((B − C)/2) = ((b − c)/(b + c)) cot(A/2).","Useful checks: the angles add up to 180°, the longest side is opposite the largest angle, and the sum of any two sides is greater than the third. If sin B comes out greater than 1 in the sine rule, no triangle exists.","Common mistake: using the sine rule when the known angle is not opposite a known side, and forgetting that sin θ = sin(180° − θ) gives two possible angles."],"e":"Sides 13, 14, 15. s = 21. Area = √(21 × 8 × 7 × 6) = 84. The circumradius is R = abc/(4Δ) = 2730/336 = 8.125. cos A = (14² + 15² − 13²)/(2 × 14 × 15) = 252/420 = 0.6."},"3.3":{"s":"Trigonometric functions are not one-one over their whole domain, so their inverses are defined on a chosen interval: the principal value branch. For example sin⁻¹ takes values in [−π/2, π/2], cos⁻¹ in [0, π] and tan⁻¹ in (−π/2, π/2). The chapter gives the domains and ranges, the rules for negative arguments, and addition formulas such as the one for tan⁻¹x + tan⁻¹y.","f":[{"x":"sin^-1(x) + cos^-1(x) = pi/2","n":"For -1 <= x <= 1; also tan^-1(x) + cot^-1(x) = pi/2 for all real x.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.96"},{"x":"sin^-1(-x) = -sin^-1(x)","n":"Odd functions: also tan^-1(-x) = -tan^-1(x).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.96"},{"x":"cos^-1(-x) = pi - cos^-1(x)","n":"For -1 <= x <= 1; likewise cot^-1(-x) = pi - cot^-1(x).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.96"},{"x":"tan^-1(x) + tan^-1(y) = tan^-1((x + y)/(1 - x*y))","n":"For x > 0, y > 0 and xy < 1; if xy > 1 add pi.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.96"},{"x":"tan^-1(x) - tan^-1(y) = tan^-1((x - y)/(1 + x*y))","n":"For x > 0, y > 0.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.97"}],"w":["sin⁻¹(sin 5π/6) is π/6, not 5π/6: the answer must lie in the principal range.","sin⁻¹x is the inverse function, not 1/sin x."],"x":["Sine, cosine and tangent are not one-one on all of R, so each inverse is defined on a chosen branch, the principal value. sin⁻¹ x has range [−π/2, π/2] for x in [−1, 1]. cos⁻¹ x has range [0, π] for x in [−1, 1]. tan⁻¹ x has range (−π/2, π/2) for all real x. The notation sin⁻¹ means the inverse function, not 1/sin.","Always answer within the principal range. For example, sin⁻¹(sin 5π/6) = π/6, because sin(5π/6) = 1/2, and the angle in [−π/2, π/2] with sine 1/2 is π/6.","Key properties: sin⁻¹(−x) = −sin⁻¹ x and tan⁻¹(−x) = −tan⁻¹ x. cos⁻¹(−x) = π − cos⁻¹ x. sin⁻¹ x + cos⁻¹ x = π/2 for x in [−1, 1], and tan⁻¹ x + cot⁻¹ x = π/2 for all real x. Also sin(sin⁻¹ x) = x for x in [−1, 1], but sin⁻¹(sin x) = x only when x is in [−π/2, π/2].","Addition formulas: tan⁻¹ x + tan⁻¹ y = tan⁻¹((x + y)/(1 − xy)) when xy is less than 1. If x and y are positive and xy is greater than 1, add π. tan⁻¹ x − tan⁻¹ y = tan⁻¹((x − y)/(1 + xy)) for positive x and y. Also 2 tan⁻¹ x = tan⁻¹(2x/(1 − x²)) for x between −1 and 1.","Method for a mixed expression: give each inverse a name, such as A = sin⁻¹(3/5), draw a right triangle to find the other ratios, and use the compound-angle formula. Common mistake: forgetting the π in the addition formula."],"e":"Find sin(cos⁻¹(3/5)). Let θ = cos⁻¹(3/5), so cos θ = 3/5 with θ in [0, π/2]. Then sin θ = √(1 − 9/25) = 4/5. Another: tan⁻¹ 2 + tan⁻¹ 3 = π + tan⁻¹(−1) = 3π/4."}};
